# Lessons Learned: Building SnapRoll on Circle Wallets + Arc

While building **SnapRoll** (automated cross-border payroll on Circle User-Controlled Wallets + Arc), I ran into several bugs that I couldn't find documented anywhere else. Sharing them here in plain language, in case they save another Arc/Circle builder some debugging time.

Each entry follows: **what happened → why it happened → how I fixed it.**

---

## 1. Google login only works on your home page — until you fix this

**What happened:** Sign-in with Google worked perfectly on my app's main page. But the moment I tried adding Google login to a *different* page (like `/approve`), something strange happened: after signing in, the browser always landed back on the **home page** — never the page the user actually started from. The login itself succeeded; it just "teleported" the user to the wrong place, with no login data available on the page they meant to use.

**Why it happened:** This is a Circle Wallets + Google OAuth quirk, not a bug in my own code. Google Cloud Console only lets you register a **domain** as an authorized redirect destination (e.g. `https://yourapp.com`) — it does not let you register a specific *path* like `https://yourapp.com/approve`. Circle's SDK also only accepts a domain-level `redirectUri`. So no matter which page a user starts login from, Google *always* sends them back to the bare domain (the home page) when it's done. There is no config setting that fixes this — it's how the redirect mechanism is designed to work.

**The fix:** Hand off the login result manually between pages, using the browser's local storage as a relay:

1. **Before redirecting to Google**, save the page the user was actually on:
   ```ts
   localStorage.setItem("postLoginRedirect", window.location.pathname);
   ```
2. **On the home page**, once login succeeds, check if that value exists. If it does, don't treat the login as "for this page" — instead, stash the result and forward the browser to the original page:
   ```ts
   const redirectTo = localStorage.getItem("postLoginRedirect");
   if (redirectTo && redirectTo !== "/") {
     localStorage.setItem("pendingLoginResult", JSON.stringify(result));
     localStorage.removeItem("postLoginRedirect");
     window.location.href = redirectTo;
   }
   ```
3. **On the destination page**, when it first loads, check for that pending result *before* waiting on anything else, and use it directly:
   ```ts
   const pending = localStorage.getItem("pendingLoginResult");
   if (pending) {
     localStorage.removeItem("pendingLoginResult");
     // use this result to finish logging in on this page
   }
   ```

**The lesson:** If you're building a multi-page app with Circle Wallets + social login, do **not** assume login "just works" the same way on every page the way it does on the homepage. You need to build your own relay for the login result. I couldn't find this documented anywhere when I hit it — hopefully this saves someone else the same afternoon of confusion.

